&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&#34;description&#34;&gt;
    &lt;a href=&#34;#description&#34; class=&#34;header-anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;
    Description
&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&#34;https://pho3nix-jacki3.github.io/p/square-ctf/img/tyubsfcAKJDSDHlskfna09.jpg&#34;
	width=&#34;1523&#34;
	height=&#34;791&#34;
	
	loading=&#34;lazy&#34;
	
	
		class=&#34;gallery-image&#34; 
		data-flex-grow=&#34;192&#34;
		data-flex-basis=&#34;462px&#34;
	
&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ok, the developers got smart, and figured out a way to prevent SQLwe in the log in page. Plus, they decided the flag shouldn&amp;rsquo;t be hardcoded in the web application anymore. How could anyone get to it now?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SquareCTF Going In Blind is the second of two MySQL injection challenges. The &lt;a class=&#34;link&#34; href=&#34;https://squarectf.com/2022/alexhanlonhastheflag.html&#34;  target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;
    &gt;first challenge&lt;/a&gt; was a MySQL injection to bypass log in, where we logged in using &lt;code&gt;username=ahanlon&#39;-- -&lt;/code&gt; to get the flag.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
        </item>
        
